    "id": "mindset-evidence-check-1",
    "title": "1. Strategy Trial 1: Study, Test, Adjust (20%)",
    "name": "Strategy Trial 1: Study, Test, Adjust",
    "summary": "Choose one manageable learning task and test one strategy supported by course evidence for at least seven days. Your grade depends on how carefully you complete and explain the trial, not on whether the strategy succeeds.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">PSY355, Fall 2026. Weight: 20 percent.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one manageable learning task and test one strategy supported by course evidence for at least seven days. Your grade depends on how carefully you complete and explain the trial, not on whether the strategy succeeds.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A clear starting point: name the task, your current approach, and the evidence you will track.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name the strategy. Explain why course evidence supports trying it.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Two dated check-ins describing what you tried, what you noticed and what you changed. No measurements, calculations, charts or statistical analysis are required.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One adjustment made during the trial.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A keep, change, or stop decision with reasons.</li></ul><h3>First-year learning boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use plain-language observations and the concepts taught in this course. Do not diagnose yourself or another person, propose treatment, calculate statistics or conduct statistical analysis. This is a General Education learning task, not clinical work or a psychology research project.</p><h3>Scope and format</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Aim for 500 to 700 words in total, or a three-to-five-minute audio explanation with a transcript. Use the required parts as headings and include two brief dated check-ins. No visual, research paper, statistical test or clinical assessment is required.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Try the strategy for at least seven calendar days within the assignment window. Choose a low-risk task such as practising a skill or studying course material. Use one assigned source and explain one relevant course concept. A small trial of your own learning cannot prove that a strategy works for everyone.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>starting point and design: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The task, starting point, strategy, and rationale form a clear test.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The main trial design is clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: The starting point or strategy logic is thin.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The trial cannot be understood or evaluated.</li></ul><h3>Dated evidence: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Two or more useful evidence points and an adjustment are documented.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: Required evidence and adjustment are present.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Evidence is partial or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: Process evidence is absent or simulated.</li></ul><h3>Analysis: 6 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The result is interpreted accurately using course evidence and limits.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The result and course connection are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Analysis is mostly descriptive or overclaims.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The conclusion is unsupported.</li></ul><h3>Decision and reflection: 4 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The keep, change, or stop decision is specific, honest, and actionable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The decision fits the evidence.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: The decision is vague or weakly supported.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: No meaningful decision is made.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write or paste your written work into the Submission editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose a visual format, insert it with a text description or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Submit, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: September 18, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: September 25, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends September 27, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
