    "title": "4. Strategy Trial 2: Recovery and Help-Seeking (20%)",
    "name": "Strategy Trial 2: Recovery and Help-Seeking",
    "summary": "Run a second trial focused on recovery, psychological flexibility (adapting when circumstances change), self-compassion, communication, or asking for help. Use a different situation or strategy from Trial 1.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">PSY355, Fall 2026. Weight: 20 percent.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Run a second trial focused on recovery, psychological flexibility (adapting when circumstances change), self-compassion, communication, or asking for help. Use a different situation or strategy from Trial 1.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A starting plan linked to a course concept.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Two dated evidence points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One adjustment during the trial.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A reflection on how the situation supported or limited the strategy.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A realistic next action and an appropriate source of support.</li></ul><h3>Boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use a low-risk situation. This is not therapy or a clinical assessment. When a situation exceeds personal coping, name a suitable professional or Seneca support service instead of proposing self-treatment.</p><h3>First-year learning boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use plain-language observations and the concepts taught in this course. Do not diagnose yourself or another person, propose treatment, calculate statistics or conduct statistical analysis. This is a General Education learning task, not clinical work or a psychology research project.</p><h3>Scope and format</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Aim for 500 to 700 words in total, or a three-to-five-minute audio explanation with a transcript. Use the required parts as headings and include two brief dated check-ins. No visual, research paper, statistical test or clinical assessment is required.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Try one low-risk strategy for at least seven calendar days. Choose a strategy already taught in Weeks 7 to 9. Week 11 help-seeking ideas may inform your conclusion, but you do not have to restart the trial or test material that has not yet been taught. Use one assigned source and one course concept; do not collect another person’s data.</p><h3>Build on earlier feedback</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Before you start, revisit the feedback on Assessment 3. Choose one suggestion that can help your reasoning or use of evidence here. You do not need a grade or feedback on an assignment that is still being marked.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Context and plan: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The trial fits a manageable situation and uses an appropriate concept and support route.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The plan is clear and appropriate.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: The plan or context is vague.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The plan is unsafe, clinical, or cannot be evaluated.</li></ul><h3>Dated process evidence: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Two useful evidence points and an adjustment are documented.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: Required evidence is present.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Evidence is partial or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: Process evidence is absent or simulated.</li></ul><h3>Analysis of context: 6 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The reflection explains how personal, relational, and environmental factors shaped the result.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The main contextual factors are analysed.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Context is mentioned but thinly analysed.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The result is individualized or unsupported.</li></ul><h3>Next action: 4 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The next action is specific, realistic, and includes appropriate help-seeking.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The next action fits the evidence.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: The next action is vague or weakly supported.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: No usable next action is provided.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write or paste your written work into the Submission editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose a visual format, insert it with a text description or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Submit, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: November 9, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: November 27, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends November 29, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
