    "name": "Private Learning Journal",
    "summary": "Complete six dated entries across Weeks 2 to 12. Begin each entry with a real learning moment, item from class, or strategy attempt. Then use one idea from psychology covered in this course to explain what happened.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Complete six dated entries across Weeks 2 to 12. Begin each entry with a real learning moment, item from class, or strategy attempt. Then use one idea from psychology covered in this course to explain what happened.</p><h3>Each entry must include</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A date and specific situation.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">The strategy, decision, or response you tried.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One course concept, explained accurately.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Evidence of change, no change, or uncertainty.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One next action.</li></ul><h3>Format and privacy</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Aim for 100 to 150 words or about a one-minute audio entry with a transcript. The journal is private. You do not need to disclose trauma, diagnosis, health details, or another person’s information. Personal disclosure is never graded.</p><h3>First-year learning boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use plain-language observations and the concepts taught in this course. Do not diagnose yourself or another person, propose treatment, calculate statistics or conduct statistical analysis. This is a General Education learning task, not clinical work or a psychology research project.</p><h3>Keep the journal useful and brief</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use three entries from the first half and three from the second half. Aim to complete your first three by October 7 so you can use them in the mid-course reflection. This is a planning target, not an extra graded deadline. A few clear sentences can address the five prompts. Do not reproduce a full strategy-trial report in an entry.</p><h3>How the journal is graded</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Dated evidence of your learning process: 2.5 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">accurate use of course ideas: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">reflection and adjustment: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">communication and privacy: 1.5 points.</li></ul><h3>Plan your six entries</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Complete at least three entries before the Mid-course Reflection so you have dated evidence to use there. Continue with three more entries in the second half of the course. These are planning steps within one graded journal, not extra assignments.</p><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this journal in Blackboard and find Entries. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Post, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries through the submission window stated below. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: September 14, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: December 2, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends December 4, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
