    "id": "personal-resilience-plan",
    "title": "6. Personal Resilience Plan (15%)",
    "name": "Personal Resilience Plan",
    "summary": "Build a practical learning and well-being support plan from three completed pieces of your course work and feedback already returned. Choose evidence from more than one type of task. You do not need the grades on Trial 2 or the final assignments.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 15% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 15 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">PSY355, Fall 2026. Weight: 15 percent.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Build a practical learning and well-being support plan from three completed pieces of your course work and feedback already returned. Choose evidence from more than one type of task. You do not need the grades on Trial 2 or the final assignments.</p><h3>Your plan includes</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Early signs that your learning is becoming stuck.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Specific actions you can take before, during, and after a difficult period.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">People, services, and environmental changes that can support you.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A repeating process for planning, checking your progress, making changes, and reviewing the result.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Three specific dated examples from your own course work, with evidence from both halves of the term.</li></ul><h3>Format and boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose a 500 to 700 word plan or a three-to-five-minute narrated plan with a transcript. This is not a diagnosis, crisis plan, or treatment plan. Private disclosure is not required.</p><h3>First-year learning boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use plain-language observations and the concepts taught in this course. Do not diagnose yourself or another person, propose treatment, calculate statistics or conduct statistical analysis. This is a General Education learning task, not clinical work or a psychology research project.</p><h3>Build on earlier feedback</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Before you start, revisit the feedback on Assessment 3. Choose one suggestion that can help your reasoning or use of evidence here. You do not need a grade or feedback on an assignment that is still being marked.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Course-long evidence: 4.5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Three or more dated course items from both halves form a clear evidence base.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: Required course items are present and connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Evidence is thin, clustered, or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The plan lacks required course evidence.</li></ul><h3>Plan specificity: 4.5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Signs, actions, supports, environment, and review loop are concrete and usable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The main plan components are clear and practical.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Several components are generic or incomplete.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The plan is too vague to use.</li></ul><h3>Concept integration: 3.75 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Psychological concepts are accurate, balanced, and connected to context and limits.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: Concepts are accurate and relevant.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Concept use is partial or mostly labelled.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: Concepts are absent or substantially inaccurate.</li></ul><h3>Communication and boundaries: 2.25 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The plan is coherent, accessible, and respects learning and health boundaries.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: Communication and boundaries are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Clarity or boundaries are uneven.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The work is difficult to follow or crosses clinical boundaries.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write or paste your written work into the Submission editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose a visual format, insert it with a text description or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Submit, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: November 23, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: December 11, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">No automatic 48-hour window applies to this final assessment. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Work submitted after the posted deadline receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
